# Red Fajita Marinade (Carne Asada)

Source: ArnieTex, "The SECRET RED MARINADE Recipe for CARNE ASADA used by Mexican Meat Markets & Restaurants." https://youtu.be/eUfZBIMeNNk

## Recipe Name:
Red Fajita Marinade (Texas meat-market style, for carne asada / skirt steak)

## Servings:
Marinade batch suits about 4 to 4 1/2 lb of skirt steak (two strips at roughly 2 lb each). He says "might even be five."

## Prep Time:
10 minutes

## Cook Time:
6 to 10 minutes on the grill (2 to 4 or 5 minutes per side)

## Ingredients:
### Liquids
- 1/4 cup fresh orange juice
- 1/4 cup fresh pineapple juice
- 1/2 cup lime juice
- 1 tbsp apple cider vinegar
- 1/4 cup olive oil

### Seasonings
- 1 tbsp salt
- 1 tbsp black pepper
- 1 tsp granulated garlic
- 1 tsp granulated onion
- 1 tsp dried Mexican oregano
- 1/2 tsp ground cumin
- 2 tbsp smoked paprika
- 2 tsp chili powder
- 3 tbsp achiote paste

Use achiote in brick form if you can. He says the bricks carry a few extra ingredients that add flavor. His on-screen card says "achiote paste" while he calls it powder or brick; both work.

### Meat
- 2 skirt steaks, about 2 lb each

### To finish
- Extra seasoning rub for the grill (he uses a "WOW" rub made for steaks and fajitas)
- Flour tortillas
- Cheese for tostadas (Oaxaca style, or Longhorn-style cheddar)
- Salsa, red and green

## Instructions:
1. Put every marinade ingredient in a blender. Everything is powder or liquid, so a blender is optional, but it mixes faster.
2. Blend 20 to 30 seconds, just long enough to combine.
3. Pour some marinade in the bottom of a glass bowl. Lay in the steaks and rub the marinade in. Add more on top and rub again. Do not drown the meat.
4. Cover with plastic wrap and refrigerate.
5. Marinate thin cuts (fajitas, steaks, chops) 2 to 4 hours at the low end. Overnight is much better. For a whole chicken, big beef ribs, pork butt, or chuck roast, go at least 8 to 10 hours, overnight best.
6. Start the fire. To reuse charcoal, shut both air vents right after the last cook; the fire dies fast and the charcoal is good again.
7. Fill the chimney short of full when charcoal is already in the grill. Light it, dump it, then spread the coals. Leave a little empty room on one side.
8. Add a couple of pieces of mesquite. Open the bottom inlet all the way at first so the wood catches and burns hot instead of smoldering. Set the inlet halfway once it is going. Grill ran at 550 F to start.
9. Clean the grates with a half onion. Cut slits in a crisscross pattern all the way around, spear it, press hard, and run it across the grates a couple of times. Then drop the onion half on the coals.
10. Lay the steaks on. Shake extra rub over them; the marinade salt alone is not enough.
11. Flip after about 3 minutes, and keep flipping over a close hot fire (his "flippity flip" method). Shake on more rub.
12. Flip again as soon as moisture beads on top. Do not let that moisture evaporate. Heat pushes it up from the bottom, so the flip keeps it in the meat.
13. Cook 2 to 4 or 5 minutes per side, by feel rather than a timer. Fire read 425 F during this stage.
14. Pull them. One strip was at 135 to 136 F, the other at 157 to 167 F. At that high heat, 167 F still ate like medium to medium-well, not well done.
15. Move the fire to one side. Lay flour tortillas on the grill for about 5 minutes, until toasty.
16. Shred the cheese. Put cheese on the tortillas, slow the fire down, and melt it. These are tostadas.
17. Fold a steak over and slice it about 4 to 5 inches long, roughly the length of the tortilla. Slice with the grain first, then across the grain.
18. Put sliced meat on tortillas or tostadas. Spoon the juices from the cutting board over the top. Add salsa.

